" decoding=\"async\" data-nimg=\"fill\" class=\"object-cover\" style=\"position:absolute;height:100%;width:100%;left:0;top:0;right:0;bottom:0;color:transparent\" sizes=\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\" srcSet=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_7177.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>[Remaining details - Shri Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath Temple, Kheda]</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station to Kheda, on the Ahmedabad-Bombay highway, is Nadiad, 18 kilometers away. Bus and taxi services are available here. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available. There is an upashraya, a pathshala and an ayambilshala here. From here, Dholka Tirth is 30 km, Matar Tirth (Shri Saccha Sumatinath) is 7 km, Manilakshmi Tirth is 43 km, and Khambhat is 56 km away.</p><p>Stuti: &quot;Amid the crowd (bheed) of vices I stand bewildered in this worldly existence; O Bhidbhanjan, dispel the crowd of troubles for your devotee. Manifesting truly beneath the banyan tree, Kheda Tirth shines forth. To Shri Bhidbhanjan Prabhu Parshwa, with devotion, I offer my vandana.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Arham Shri Bhidbhanjan Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in &quot;Shri Shri 365 Parshwajin Naammala,&quot; &quot;Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Bhatev Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Naammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Jambuswami Ras,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaityaparipati,&quot; &quot;Malaysundari Mahabal Ras,&quot; and others. There are many temples of Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath across India. One idol is at the Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath temple at Jiravala Tirth, another at the Kalikund Parshwanath Mandir in Santacruz, Mumbai, and another at the Gujarati Jainwadi in Chennai.</p><p>Trust: Shri Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath Jain Shwetambar Tirth Pedhi, Patelvado, near the Main Post Office, Post: Kheda, District: Kheda - 387411, State of Gujarat, India. Phone: 02694-222012, Mobile: 9924838862.</p><p>Parshwanath 16 of 108 - Shri Kalikund Parshwanath</p><p>Bavla-Kheda Road, Post: Dholka, District: Ahmedabad - 387810.</p><p>Main Deity: An idol of Lord Shri Kalikund Parshwanath, about 120 centimeters tall, white in color with a beautifully carved parikar, in the padmasana posture, with a canopy of 9 serpent hoods above its head.</p><p>Location: This temple is in the town of Dholka on the Vadodara-Palitana road.</p><p>History: In ancient times this town was known as Dhavalakpur. It is believed that during the time of the Pandavas, this town was called Viratnagar. During the time of Lord Parshwanath, three tirthas came into being at their source, one of which is Kalikund Tirth. There is a story behind the origin of this tirtha. Lord Parshwanath was standing in kaussagga meditation at the foot of Kali Parvat in the Kadambari forest near Champanagari. Seeing the Lord, the elephant Mahidhar recalled his previous birth. He went to the Kund lake, brought lotus flowers from there, offered them, and worshipped the Lord. On hearing news of the Lord&#x27;s arrival, King Karkandu came to the forest, but by the time the king arrived, the Lord had already left that place for elsewhere. The king fell into despair over his misfortune and praised the good fortune of the elephant. To lift the king out of his despondency, the gods fashioned a striking idol of Lord Parshwanath at nine cubits in height (the Lord&#x27;s original height) and gave it to the king. Seeing this beautiful and striking idol, the king was overjoyed. The king had a grand temple built and installed this idol there, worshipping it daily. This tirtha came to be known as &quot;Kalikund Tirth&quot; (Kali + Kund). The elephant died and was reborn as a mighty vyantar deity, becoming the presiding deity of this temple. This original temple no longer exists today. There are many temples and idols of Kalikund Parshwanath throughout India. The Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Dholka has recently come to light and has become a very famous tirtha. Between the 12th and 14th centuries, Dholka was a very prosperous city of historical importance. Bapu Gurudev Shri Jindutsurji was born here in Vikram Samvat 1132 and was given diksha (ordination) here by Shri Dharmadev Upadhyay in Vikram Samvat 1411. Vaghabhatt, son of Minister Udayan, built a vast temple here, called &quot;Udayan Vihar,&quot; in which an idol of Lord Simandhar Swami was installed. Under the guidance of Acharya Shri Vadidevsurji, in Vikram Samvat 1276, King Virdhaval appointed Vastupal Tejpal as his minister...</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-017\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Bhuvan Parshwanath and Shri Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-019\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Kalikund Parshwanath (Dholka) and Shri Shamla Parshwanath (Ahmedabad)</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\"><p>१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन</p><p>Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur</p></div></footer><!--$--><!--/$--><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/40